Accepted deviation. `\xo 1.1 \em \+pn name\+pn* stuff \em*`: Paratext closes
`\xo` at the un-plussed `\em` and marks the `\+pn` inside it
`status="invalid"`. The parser nests `\em` under `\xo`, as it does for any
style that may nest (`NEST`) and is closed by its own marker ahead, and
reports `character-style-nested-without-plus`. The stylesheet gives no rule
that separates this case from `\ft ... \sc BC\sc* ...` (specExamples/
extended/contentCatogories1), where the reference nests, so the parser keeps
the one rule. The `status` attribute is Paratext's way of reporting; the
parser reports through diagnostics.

--- origin.xml
+++ origin.xml
@@ -3,9 +3,8 @@
   <chapter number="1" style="c" sid="GEN 1" />
   <para style="p">
     <note caller="+" style="x">
-      <char style="xo">1.1 </char>
-      <char style="em">
-        <char style="pn" status="invalid">name</char> stuff </char>
+      <char style="xo">1.1 <char style="em">
+        <char style="pn">name</char> stuff </char></char>
     </note> <verse number="1" style="v" sid="GEN 1:1" />something<verse eid="GEN 1:1" /></para>
   <chapter eid="GEN 1" />
 </usx>
